{
    "title": "Agency Information Collection Activities; Submission for OMB Review; Comment Request; Notice of Termination, Peace Corps Volunteer Authorization for Examination and/or Treatment",
    "type": "Notice",
    "date": "December 02, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-02/pdf/C1-2025-19133.pdf",
    "summary": "Today\u2019s Federal Register bundles a few housekeeping fixes with a notable invitation for public input on nuclear plant license changes. It updates timelines, corrects earlier notices, and explains how people can access certain sensitive documents to take part in the process.\n\nFirst, the U.S. International Trade Commission has paused its schedule in an ongoing trade proceeding by 47 days. That means decisions and deadlines in that case will shift accordingly. The notice cites the Tariff Act of 1930 and the Commission\u2019s rules as the basis for the change.\n\nSecond, the Department of Justice corrected a web link so people can find proposed consent decrees in the right place. If you rely on those documents, the correct page is on the Justice Department site under \u201cenvironment and natural resources\u201d consent decrees. Third, the Department of Labor corrected a date in an earlier notice about a Peace Corps volunteer medical authorization form. The deadline that had been listed as December 31, 2025 should have been October 31, 2025.\n\nThe biggest new item comes from the Nuclear Regulatory Commission. It is considering two license amendment requests for Indian Point Station (Units 1, 2, and 3) and Salem Nuclear Generating Station (Units 1 and 2). The agency proposes to find that the changes do not present significant new safety concerns. Because the requests include sensitive but not classified information, the Commission has set procedures for people who want to request a hearing or intervene to seek access to those materials for preparing their arguments. Comments are due January 2, 2026. Requests for a hearing or to intervene are due February 2, 2026. Anyone who thinks they need access to the sensitive documents must ask for it by December 12, 2025.\n\nWho benefits? Businesses, lawyers, and advocates who track trade cases gain clarity on timing. Communities near Indian Point and Salem, as well as plant workers and owners, get a clear path and timeline to weigh in on proposed license changes. The corrected Justice Department link helps the public and practitioners find settlement documents more easily. The Labor Department\u2019s date correction clarifies the record for those following the Peace Corps form review.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Parties in the trade case who were counting on a faster resolution will face delay from the 47-day pause. People who planned to comment on the Peace Corps notice based on the later date may find they missed the window. For would-be participants in the nuclear license cases, the need to request access to sensitive materials and meet tight deadlines could add steps and pressure. Still, clearer instructions and firm dates may ultimately make participation more straightforward for those who act promptly.",
